{
"dur": 67.08,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 7.64,
"text": "Think about what makes buying drugs on a street corner risky. The seller might rob you. Or the police might be watching.",
"end": 7.915
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 7.915,
"vo_start": 8.19,
"vo_end": 13.189,
"text": "Silk Road tried to remove both risks by copying the tricks that make Amazon and eBay feel safe.",
"end": 13.464
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 13.464,
"vo_start": 13.739,
"vo_end": 21.253,
"text": "One: ratings. Every seller had reviews from buyers. Sell bad goods, and one-star reviews would sink your business.",
"end": 21.528
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 21.528,
"vo_start": 21.803,
"vo_end": 29.803,
"text": "Two: escrow. The buyer's bitcoins didn't go straight to the seller. The site held them until the buyer confirmed the order had arrived.",
"end": 30.078
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 30.078,
"vo_start": 30.353,
"vo_end": 37.113,
"text": "Three: the post. Orders went by ordinary mail, packed to look like everyday parcels, lost among millions of real ones.",
"end": 37.388
},
{
"i": 6,
"start": 37.388,
"vo_start": 37.663,
"vo_end": 39.771,
"text": "And on every sale, the site took a cut.",
"end": 40.046
},
{
"i": 7,
"start": 40.046,
"vo_start": 40.321,
"vo_end": 49.16,
"text": "It banned a few things, like stolen credit cards and child abuse material. But most listings were drugs, and fake IDs and hacking tools were sold too.",
"end": 49.435
},
{
"i": 8,
"start": 49.435,
"vo_start": 49.71,
"vo_end": 58.35,
"text": "In June 2011, the website Gawker published a story about it. Within days, Senator Chuck Schumer called a press conference and demanded it be shut down.",
"end": 58.625
},
{
"i": 9,
"start": 58.625,
"vo_start": 58.9,
"vo_end": 61.06,
"text": "The headlines made Silk Road famous.",
"end": 61.335
},
{
"i": 10,
"start": 61.335,
"vo_start": 61.61,
"vo_end": 66.13,
"text": "Now the government was paying attention. And the man running the site needed a new name.",
"end": 67.08
}
],
